  "verbalization": "Carmustine, a chemotherapy agent, is linked to Alk-positive Large B-cell Lymphoma through a chain of indications involving Bleomycin. The evidence suggests that Carmustine is used to treat Diffuse Large B-cell Lymphoma of the Central Nervous System, Plasmablastic Lymphoma, and Primary Central Nervous System Lymphoma, all of which are associated with Bleomycin. Bleomycin, in turn, is indicated for Alk-positive Large B-cell Lymphoma, indicating a potential pathway where Carmustine may influence the disease through the use of Bleomycin. The shared use of Bleomycin across these conditions implies a possible mechanism of action or therapeutic relationship that could be relevant to Alk-positive Large B-cell Lymphoma. The evidence supports a connection between Carmustine and Alk-positive Large B-cell Lymphoma through the indirect pathway involving Bleomycin, highlighting a possible role in the treatment of this lymphoma subtype.",
